      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "34bf0d5b-6066-5a2f-938c-fb2dc4bc4376",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gabriela Mistral, Alan Sillitoe, Geoffrey Chaucer, Thomas Wyatt, Ivo Andrić, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, Octavio Paz, Theodor Mommsen\n- Topics: Nobel Prize in Literature, poet-diplomats, angry young men\n- Organizations: Prussian Academy of Sciences\n- Works: A History of Rome\n\nFacts:\n- In 1902, Theodor Mommsen received the Nobel Prize in Literature for his historical writing, especially A History of Rome, after 18 members of the Prussian Academy of Sciences nominated him.\n- In 1945, Gabriela Mistral became the first Latin American author to receive the Nobel Prize in Literature.\n- Alan Sillitoe, born on 4 March 1928 and died on 25 April 2010, was an English writer associated with the “angry young men” of the 1950s.\n- Alan Sillitoe disliked the “angry young men” label, as did most writers to whom it was applied.\n- Geoffrey Chaucer and Thomas Wyatt are among the best-known poet-diplomats.\n- The poet-diplomat category includes Nobel Prize in Literature recipients Ivo Andrić, Gabriela Mistral, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, and Octavio Paz.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "93ef26fe-e3c7-5ae8-890f-5f2fd086ad25",
        "text": "Page: Gabriela Mistral. Sentence: In 1945 she became the first Latin American author to receive a Nobel Prize in Literature, \"for her lyric poetry which, inspired by powerful emotions, has made her name a symbol of the idealistic aspirations of the entire Latin American world\".\nPage: Alan Sillitoe. Sentence: Alan Sillitoe (4 March 192825 April 2010) was an English writer and one of the so-called \"angry young men\" of the 1950s.\nPage: Alan Sillitoe. Sentence: He disliked the label, as did most of the other writers to whom it was applied.\nPage: Poet-diplomat. Sentence: The best known poet-diplomats are perhaps Geoffrey Chaucer and Thomas Wyatt; the category also includes recipients of the Nobel Prize in Literature: Ivo Andrić, Gabriela Mistral, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz and Octavio Paz.\nPage: Theodor Mommsen. Sentence: He received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1902 for being \"the greatest living master of the art of historical writing, with special reference to his monumental work \"A History of Rome\"\", after having been nominated by 18 members of the Prussian Academy of Sciences.",
